[h1]Using a HPC on a Daily Basis[/h1]

Recently I've started using my [url=http://www.hpcfactor.com/reviews/hardware/hp/jornada720/]Jornada 720[/url] as a replacement for my iPad as a lab computer since it's extremely tiny (space is very important in the bench) and can do practically everything I need my iPad for, of course I still have my main computer in the lab to program microcontrollers and do everything, but the Jornada is great to have near my working area so I can quickly check datasheets, schematics, and parts list while soldering or prototyping.

[img=<^base_url^>/I/<^reldir^>/lab-view.jpg]My workbench and lab space[/img]

Usually when I'm prototyping or testing a board I have to check datasheets for pinouts, common voltages, etc. and Adobe Reader is great for this task:

[img=<^base_url^>/I/<^reldir^>/datasheet.png]Datasheet opened on Acrobat for Windows CE[/img]

In my main computer I keep a very well organized folder (and database, but I'm still researching the best way to get the DB into my j720 and keep them both synced) with datasheets for practically every electronic component I have in stock. So whenever I update the folder with more stuff I just [url=http://rsync.samba.org/]rsync[/url] everything to my Jornada, and since it's a nice Linux machine I've already wrote a nice script to automate things.

[img=<^base_url^>/I/<^reldir^>/folders.png]Datasheet category folders[/img]

[img=<^base_url^>/I/<^reldir^>/datasheet-folder.png]Inside an category folder[/img]

Last, but certainly not least, I've built a small command line application a while ago called [url=https://github.com/nathanpc/build-bom]build-bom[/url] which gets a schematic file from my CAD program ([url=http://www.cadsoftusa.com/]EAGLE[/url]) and can output the parts list into HTML, CSV, or JSON. So whenever I'm populating a board I export the parts list to HTML and open it in my Jornada so I can know which part to place where in the board:

[img=<^base_url^>/I/<^reldir^>/parts-list.png]HTML parts list generated by build-bom[/img]
